{"id": "LY-R-repository-commands", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "provisional", "text": "kairos-checks runs each repository's own declared test commands (repository-commands module): argv lists in the adapter, programs limited by the central suite's allowed_programs, pass on exit 0, output never copied into the report; optional per suite so undeclared repos report not-declared, and a gate needs at least one passed module", "rationale": "Zach 2026-11-12 07:47 CT: 'RK seems not to be running the Kairos checks correctly'. The required check ran 207 unittest cases and skipped relay-kit's 349-case tests/run_tests.py, node browser tests and prepublish scan, which only a non-required workflow ran", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@3"}]}
{"id": "LY-R-hook-guard", "version": 1, "type": "requirement", "subject": "design", "epistemic": "provisional", "text": "kairos-checks holds every repository's agent hook commands (.claude/settings.json, committed settings.local.json) to SHA-256 fingerprints kept in the central suite (shared '*' or per repository) and fails on root files or folders that would shadow a module a hook imports; runs with no adapter entry; reports fingerprints, never hook text", "rationale": "Zach 2026-11-12 08:41 CT: 'why can't we put that as a CI check? That doesn't live in the branches. That lives on the repo itself... Just don't pollute anyone else's.' Follows Telosmith#77 (planted-module hook hijack). Limits: can't vet code a hook legitimately runs from the checkout; can't stop a PR that rewrites its own caller workflow", "relations": [{"rel": "serves", "to": "P0@3"}]}
